---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const EFFECTIVE = '2 November 2026';
const CONTACT = 'privacy@kaihuman.com';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy policy — kaihuman"
  description="How the kaihuman console, browser extension, desktop palette and CLI handle your data: your content stays in your own GitHub repositories."
>
  <main class="policy">
    <a href="/" class="policy__home">← kaihuman</a>
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="policy__meta">Effective {EFFECTIVE}</p>

    <h2>In short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your notes, todos, bookmarks and other content are stored as files in <strong>your own
        GitHub repositories</strong>. kaihuman has no server that stores your content, with one optional exception: if you share a
        business document by link, the encrypted file is stored on the share service (see
        “Share links” below).
      </li>
      <li>
        The apps talk directly to GitHub from your browser or computer, using your GitHub access
        token. The token stays on your device.
      </li>
      <li>There are no analytics, no tracking and no advertising, and nothing is sold.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      kaihuman is operated by Adam Urban, a private individual. For anything about privacy, write to
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What this policy covers</h2>
    <p>
      The website kaihuman.com, the web console at app.kaihuman.com, the kaihuman browser
      extension, the desktop palette and the command-line tool (CLI).
    </p>

    <h2>Signing in with GitHub</h2>
    <p>
      You sign in with GitHub. A small sign-in service exchanges the one-time code from GitHub for
      an access token and hands it straight back to your browser. It does not store the token or
      your content. GitHub shows you which access kaihuman asks for before you agree; the apps
      need read and write access to the repository you use with kaihuman.
    </p>
    <p>You can revoke the access at any time in your GitHub settings under Applications.</p>

    <h2>What is stored where</h2>
    <h3>In your GitHub repositories</h3>
    <p>
      Everything you create: notes, todos, logs, bookmarks, read-later items, knowledge-base
      entries and settings. GitHub stores it under
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
        >GitHub's privacy statement</a
      >. You can read, change, export or delete it directly on GitHub.
    </p>

    <h3>On your device</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Web console:</strong> the access token and your selected repository in the
        browser's local storage, and a cache of your GitHub profile and files in the browser's
        IndexedDB. Signing out removes the token.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Browser extension:</strong> the access token, the selected repository and caches
        (bookmarks, usage counts, today view) in the extension's local storage. The extension
        receives the token only from the console at app.kaihuman.com.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Desktop palette:</strong> the access token and an optional DeepL key in your
        operating system's keyring; settings and caches in your user configuration folders.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>CLI:</strong> the access token and a copy of your repository in your user
        configuration folder.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the browser extension reads</h2>
    <p>
      The extension has no standing access to the websites you visit. It loads into a tab only when
      you act on it there — the keyboard shortcut, the toolbar button or the right-click menu — and
      it reads a page only then: when you save the page to read later or to your
      knowledge base, it takes the page's title, address, main text and, if you allow it, its
      images, and writes them to your repository. It does not collect your browsing history. Access
      to your browser bookmarks is requested only when you import them.
    </p>

    <h2>Services you can choose to use</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Translation:</strong> if you set your own DeepL API key, the text you translate is
        sent to DeepL under DeepL's terms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI providers:</strong> the CLI can pass a prompt to an AI command-line tool you have
        installed yourself (for example Claude, Copilot or Gemini). That tool's provider handles the
        prompt under its own terms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Push reminders:</strong> if you turn on reminders, your browser's push subscription
        is stored in your repository, and a GitHub Actions workflow in your repository sends the
        reminders through your browser vendor's push service.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Share links (business documents)</h2>
    <p>
      This is optional and off until you accept an explanation in the console. When you choose to
      share an invoice, offer or credit note, the console encrypts the PDF in your browser and
      uploads only the encrypted file to the share service at docs.kaihuman.com. The key is in
      the link, after the “#”, which browsers do not send to servers, so the operator cannot read
      the file. Anyone who has the whole link can open it until it expires or you revoke it.
    </p>
    <h3>What the share service stores</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>the encrypted file, its label (for example the document number) and type;</li>
      <li>the business name from your profile, shown on the download page;</li>
      <li>the file size, the creation date and the expiry date;</li>
      <li>your numeric GitHub user id, to tell your files apart and apply limits;</li>
      <li>for each download: the time, the link's identifier and a coarse client class
        (browser, bot or other).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The service does not store IP addresses, full browser identifiers or your GitHub token. The
      token is sent to GitHub to find out who you are, and not kept. Cloudflare, which runs the
      service, processes technical request data such as IP addresses to deliver and protect it.
    </p>
    <h3>Where and how long</h3>
    <p>
      Files are kept in Cloudflare R2 in the EU jurisdiction, the records in Cloudflare D1. They
      stay until you revoke the link, which deletes the file, or delete all your shared files in the
      console, which removes the files and records. The records of a revoked link are kept so your
      list stays correct.
    </p>
    <h3>Abuse</h3>
    <p>
      Every download page has a “Report abuse” link to
      <a href="mailto:abuse@kaihuman.com">abuse@kaihuman.com</a>. The operator cannot read the
      files, so a report is handled by disabling the link.
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      kaihuman.com and app.kaihuman.com are static websites hosted by Cloudflare, which processes
      technical request data such as IP addresses to deliver and protect them. This website loads
      its fonts from Google Fonts, which receives your IP address when the page loads.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Your content is in your repositories, so you control it fully. If you are in the EU or UK,
      you also have the right to access, correct, delete or export personal data, to restrict or
      object to its processing, and to complain to a data protection authority. Write to
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a> to exercise them.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      When this policy changes, the new version is published here with a new effective date. The
      history of this page is public in the
      <a href="https://github.com/kaihuman-hq/kaihuman">kaihuman source repository</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .policy {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-md) var(--space-3xl);
  }

  .policy__home {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .policy__meta {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .policy h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    margin-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .policy h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    margin-block: var(--space-xl) var(--space-xs);
  }

  .policy h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    margin-block: var(--space-md) var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .policy ul {
    padding-left: var(--space-md);
    list-style: disc;
  }

  .policy li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .policy p,
  .policy li {
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }

  .policy a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
</style>
